questions.stem:
Seats are numbered from 1 to 5 while the array is indexed from 0, so the loop counter has to be shifted. What prints?
int[] a = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
int sum = 0;
for (int k = 1; k <= 5; k++) {
    sum = sum + a[k - 1];
}
System.out.println(sum);

The body only ever needs the values and never a position, so the loop variable is the element itself. What prints?
int[] a = {4, 8, 15};
int sum = 0;
for (int x : a) {
    sum = sum + x;
}
System.out.println(sum);

Which loop variable is the right choice for a body whose only job is to add each reading into a running total?
int[] temps = {18, 24, 31};
int total = 0;
for (int t : temps) {
    total = total + t;
}
System.out.println(total);

A cashier intends to total the whole range 1 to 10, but the bound is strict, so the last value never joins the sum. What prints?
int n = 10;
int sum = 0;
for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
    sum = sum + i;
}
System.out.println(sum);

Translate this while loop into an equivalent for header. The total must stay 6.
int i = 0;
int total = 0;
while (i < 4) {
    total = total + i;
    i++;
}

This loop must run at least once, so its test sits at the bottom of the body. Why can no for header replace it?
int n = 4;
int steps = 0;
do {
    n = -n;
    steps++;
} while (n < 0);
System.out.println(steps);

A loop peels digits off a number and adds them into seen. After every pass, seen holds the digit sum of what has already been peeled. What is printed?
int work = 4821;
int seen = 0;
while (work > 0) {
    seen = seen + work % 10;
    work = work / 10;
}
System.out.println(seen);

The for loop and the while loop below it are a translation pair. What does the printer show?
for (int i = 0; i < 7; i += 2) {
    System.out.print(i);
}
int k = 0;
while (k < 7) {
    System.out.print(k);
    k += 2;
}

A reverse traversal starts at the last index and steps down while the index is still valid. What does the printer show?
int[] a = {11, 22, 33};
for (int i = a.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    System.out.print(a[i] + " ");
}

A reverse traversal is meant to print every element, but its lower test is strict, so the element at index 0 is never reached. What shows?
int[] a = {11, 22, 33};
for (int i = a.length - 1; i > 0; i--) {
    System.out.print(a[i] + " ");
}

A loop steps by 3 from 0 for as long as the counter stays below 14. How many passes does it make?
int passes = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < 14; i += 3) {
    passes++;
}
System.out.println(passes);

One variable is doing two jobs: it is both the position and the running total, so the traversal is cut short. What prints?
int[] a = {5, 6, 7, 8};
int i = 0;
int sum = 0;
while (i < a.length) {
    sum = sum + a[i];
    i = sum;
}
System.out.println(sum);

A countdown tests before each pass, so the value that fails the test is never printed. What does the printer show?
int n = 3;
while (n > 0) {
    System.out.print(n + " ");
    n--;
}

This while loop declares its counter before the loop, so the counter is still readable once the loop ends. What prints?
int passes = 0;
int i = 0;
while (i < 3) {
    passes++;
    i++;
}
System.out.println(passes);
System.out.println(i);

Why must a while loop mention the variable it is counting in its condition? Consider a loop whose body only touches the data.
int[] a = {2, 5, 9};
int total = 0;
int i = a.length - 1;
while (i >= 0) {
    total = total + a[i];
    i--;
}

The line prints the loop state at the top of each pass. What does the printer show?
int[] a = {2, 4, 6};
int i = 0;
int sum = 0;
while (i < a.length) {
    System.out.print(i + ":" + sum + " ");
    sum = sum + a[i];
    i++;
}

A strict bound against the array length visits each index exactly once. How many passes does this loop make?
int[] a = {3, 1, 4};
int seen = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {
    seen++;
}
System.out.println(seen);

The bound was changed from strict to inclusive without noticing that a.length is a count and not the last index. What happens?
int[] a = {3, 1, 4, 1, 5};
int sum = 0;
for (int i = 0; i <= a.length; i++) {
    sum = sum + a[i];
}
System.out.println(sum);

A loop keeps the largest value seen so far, seeded from the first element. What prints?
int[] a = {12, 45, 7, 38, 21};
int best = a[0];
for (int i = 1; i < a.length; i++) {
    if (a[i] > best) {
        best = a[i];
    }
}
System.out.println(best);

The enhanced for binds each element to a local copy, so writing to the loop variable does not reach the array. What prints?
int[] a = {1, 2, 3};
for (int x : a) {
    x = x * 100;
}
System.out.println(a[0] + "," + a[1] + "," + a[2]);

A loop counts the values from start up to end with both bounds included. How many passes does it make?
int start = 7;
int end = 12;
int count = 0;
for (int n = start; n <= end; n++) {
    count++;
}
System.out.println(count);

A step of 2 combined with a strict bound never reaches the last element of the range. What prints?
int[] a = {3, 9, 2};
int i = 0;
int total = 0;
while (i < a.length) {
    total = total + a[i];
    i = i + 2;
}
System.out.println(total);

All three clauses of a for header are optional. What does this header print?
int passes = 0;
for (;;) {
    passes++;
    if (passes >= 5) {
        break;
    }
}
System.out.println(passes);

A segment of an array is written as the half-open range from start up to but not including end. Fill the blank so the sum covers indices 1 and 2 only.
int[] a = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
int end = 3;
int sum = 0;
for (int i = ____; i < end; i++) {
    sum = sum + a[i];
}
System.out.println(sum);

substring treats its end index as exclusive while the loop bound is inclusive, so the two conventions meet at the string length. What prints?
String s = "quiz";
for (int i = 1; i <= s.length(); i++) {
    System.out.print(s.substring(0, i) + "|");
}

The while loop and the first for loop agree on every pass. What total does the second for loop produce?
int n = 4;
int i = 0;
int total = 0;
while (i < n) {
    total = total + i;
    i++;
}
int other = 0;
for (int j = 0; j <= n; j++) {
    other = other + j;
}

A loop halves a working value until it reaches 1, and the invariant is that each pass divides the value by two. How many passes run?
int work = 1000;
int passes = 0;
while (work > 1) {
    work = work / 2;
    passes++;
}
System.out.println(passes);

A loop over two arrays is meant to total every element, but the inner counter starts at 1, so each array loses its first entry. What prints?
int[] a = {1, 2, 3, 4};
int[] b = {9, 8, 7, 6};
int sum = 0;
for (int pass = 0; pass < 2; pass++) {
    int[] src = pass == 0 ? a : b;
    for (int i = 1; i < src.length; i++) {
        sum = sum + src[i];
    }
}
System.out.println(sum);

A backward loop keeps the invariant that i is the number of elements still unread. What total does it print?
int[] a = {2, 5, 9};
int total = 0;
int i = a.length - 1;
while (i >= 0) {
    total = total + a[i];
    i--;
}
System.out.println(total);
